<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  tunnel.osm - a road passing under a ridge, tagged the way a mapper tags it.

  Node block 15xx, way block 150xx. Own patch around 53.3400 N, 6.2795 W.

  Three ways, one road. Way 15000 is the western approach at grade, way 15010 is
  the covered section tagged tunnel=yes layer=-1, and way 15020 is the eastern
  approach. Splitting the way at the portals is exactly how a tunnel is tagged:
  the tunnel=* tag applies to the covered stretch and to nothing else.

  Nodes 1502 and 1503 are the two portals. Each is an endpoint of every way that
  touches it, so RoadGraph keeps it as ONE node of degree 2 rather than splitting
  it per layer: the layer=-1 here describes one continuous road passing under
  something, not a grade separation. That is the same rule bridge_abutment.osm
  pins from the other side, and it is what keeps the tunnel attached to its
  approaches so the elevation solver can drop them together.

  The covered section is 200 m long and the approaches are 133 m each. The ridge
  itself is NOT in this file. There is no terrain in an .osm extract, so the test
  supplies a HeightSampler shaped around the parsed geometry, which is also the
  only way to place a ridge relative to coordinates the parser has recentred.
  See tests/road/p5_p6_fixtures.hpp, ridge_sampler().

  The ridge is deliberately SHORTER than the covered way: the hill covers about
  four fifths of the edge, so the road runs in the open for a stretch at each
  end. That is the ordinary case and it is the one the portal rule turns on. A
  tunnel=* way starts on open ground and only enters the hillside some way along,
  so the portal has to be FOUND, at the station where the road surface first
  passes TunnelConfig::min_portal_cover below the terrain, and not assumed to be
  the end of the edge.

  MIND THE UNITS. OSMParser projects through EPSG:3857 Web Mercator with no
  latitude correction, so one local unit is one true metre divided by
  cos(latitude), which is 1.6744 at 53.34 N. The covered way spans 0.003 degrees
  of longitude, which is 199 true metres and about 334 LOCAL units, and 334 is
  the number every distance in the tests is expressed in. The ridge is therefore
  sized as a FRACTION of the parsed edge length rather than in metres, so it
  cannot drift out of proportion if the fixture is ever moved.

  THE ELEVATION SOLVER DOES NOT PRODUCE THIS SHAPE, and the tests say so. Step 4
  of RoadElevationSolver drops a tunnel edge until EVERY one of its stations is
  ElevationConfig::tunnel_depth below the terrain, taking the maximum demand over
  the whole edge, so a solved tunnel is 8 m under the ground at its own ends too
  and never surfaces inside its own span. Under that solve no portal is ever
  emitted at either end. The portal tests therefore supply station heights of
  their own; see tests/road/test_structures.cpp, which states the conflict and
  names the test to delete if the solver is changed.

  Expected:
    * exactly one GraphEdge with is_tunnel set, from way 15010
    * nodes 1502 and 1503 are single GraphNodes of degree 2, and
      RoadGraph::Stats::layer_split_nodes is 0
    * against ridge_sampler() with a road held at the approach level, exactly two
      portals, each well inside its end of the tunnel edge rather than at the end
    * against a flat terrain at road level, no portal: nothing is buried
    * against a road held far below a flat terrain, no portal either: both ends
      are already buried and a headwall there is a wall across the middle of the
      tunnel
    * with no terrain sampler at all, nothing is emitted
-->
<osm version="0.6" generator="stratum-test-fixture">
  <bounds minlat="53.33960" minlon="-6.28310" maxlat="53.34040" maxlon="-6.27590"/>

  <node id="1501" version="1" lat="53.34000" lon="-6.28300"/>
  <node id="1502" version="1" lat="53.34000" lon="-6.28100"/>
  <node id="1503" version="1" lat="53.34000" lon="-6.27800"/>
  <node id="1504" version="1" lat="53.34000" lon="-6.27600"/>

  <way id="15000" version="1">
    <nd ref="1501"/>
    <nd ref="1502"/>
    <tag k="highway" v="secondary"/>
    <tag k="name" v="Killiney Hill Road"/>
    <tag k="lanes" v="2"/>
    <tag k="maxspeed" v="60"/>
    <tag k="surface" v="asphalt"/>
  </way>

  <way id="15010" version="1">
    <nd ref="1502"/>
    <nd ref="1503"/>
    <tag k="highway" v="secondary"/>
    <tag k="name" v="Killiney Hill Road"/>
    <tag k="tunnel" v="yes"/>
    <tag k="layer" v="-1"/>
    <tag k="lanes" v="2"/>
    <tag k="maxspeed" v="60"/>
    <tag k="surface" v="asphalt"/>
  </way>

  <way id="15020" version="1">
    <nd ref="1503"/>
    <nd ref="1504"/>
    <tag k="highway" v="secondary"/>
    <tag k="name" v="Killiney Hill Road"/>
    <tag k="lanes" v="2"/>
    <tag k="maxspeed" v="60"/>
    <tag k="surface" v="asphalt"/>
  </way>
</osm>
